  "schematicNotes": [
    {
      "id": "n_scope",
      "width": 390,
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 0
      },
      "text": "REAR WHEEL SPEED. RL and RR ABS drops run as two separate legs to the rear VRC's one IN M8 plug; the VRC sits mid-dash above the ECU beside the front VRC (Route 10 rev 2). One screened 4-core output cable runs to the 6-way inline DT04-6P near the ECU (IX_WS_REAR). The ECU side owns only the short spur and the DT06-6S mating half (ST185-B-cabin). No bulkhead."
    },
    {
      "id": "n_vrc",
      "width": 390,
      "color": "Orange",
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 210
      },
      "text": "VRC NOT DRAWN. The VR conditioner, its enclosure and its panel sockets belong to the enclosure build. This harness ends in exactly two M8 cable plugs, same part (binder 99 3363 100 04, 4 contacts + metal shell; Daniel 2026-10-12): m8_vrc_r_in IN - pin 1 RL VR+, 2 RL VR-, 3 RR VR+, 4 RR VR-, both sensor screens on the shell; m8_vrc_r_out OUT - pin 1 +5V, 2 Gnd Out, 3 RL out, 4 RR out, output screen on the shell. The shells bond to the VRC case. RL and RR run as separate legs and meet only at the IN plug."
    },
    {
      "id": "n_shield",
      "width": 390,
      "color": "Orange",
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 420
      },
      "text": "SHIELDS - VRC powered-device exception (docs/SHIELD-RULES.md 6.30). Each sensor screen floats at the sensor; the two input drains land together on the IN plug's metal shell (one landing). The output cable screen lands on the OUT plug's shell. Both shells bond to the VRC case through the panel sockets (360 degrees, no pigtail - plan 6.30 'Why M8'). The output screen runs on to inline pin 5 and the ECU shield ground. One screen node, grounded once, at the ECU."
    },
    {
      "id": "n_iso",
      "width": 390,
      "color": "Red",
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 630
      },
      "text": "TWO ISOLATION RULES. 1. The enclosure must not touch chassis - it is part of the screen, not a ground. Nylon mounting hardware. 2. The PCB must not touch the enclosure - nylon standoffs. The board grounds through the output cable only. If either is bonded, the screen picks up supply-ground noise and it will read as a flaky sensor."
    },
    {
      "id": "n_coat",
      "width": 390,
      "color": "Red",
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 810
      },
      "text": "MASK THE THREE CONNECTOR LANDINGS AND THE CASE RING-TERMINAL SPOT BEFORE POWDER COAT. The OUT shielding plate and the input screen ring terminals need bare metal. A coated landing silently breaks the screen."
    }
  ],
